Actually, I realise that it's kind of stupid trying to take the {be} 
outside the name.

On Friday, 18 January 2013 17:05:41 UTC, Ian Johnson wrote:
>
> {la broda be ko'a be'o noi brode} is "the one named "broda be ko'a be'o 
> noi brode"". {lo broda be ko'a ku noi brode} is "the one named "broda be 
> ko'a" which incidentally brodes". Relative clauses attached to the 
> relativized sumti themselves are still part of the name: {la broda be ko'a 
> noi brode} is "the one named "broda be ko'a noi brode"", and does not 
> assert that {ko'a brode}. 
>
> Does that answer your question? I'm not 100% sure I understand the 
> original question.
>
> mi'e la latro'a mu'o
